  "content": {
    "id": "p10-q31-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "31",
    "prompt": "Suppose $n\\in\\mathbb{N}$. Prove that if $2^n+1$ is prime, then $n=2^k$ for some non-negative integer $k$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$n=2^k$ for some integer $k\\ge0$.\n\nIf $n$ has an odd factor greater than $1$, the sum-of-odd-powers factorisation makes $2^n+1$ composite.",
      "worked": "Suppose $n$ is not a power of $2$. Then $n=ab$, where $a\\ge1$ and $b>1$ is odd. Hence\n$$\\begin{align*}\n2^n+1&=2^{ab}+1\\\\\n2^n+1&=(2^a)^b+1\\\\\n2^n+1&=(2^a+1)\\left(2^{a(b-1)}-2^{a(b-2)}+\\cdots-2^a+1\\right).\n\\end{align*}$$\nThe first factor is at least $3$. The second factor is an integer greater than $1$, since $2^{ab}+1>2^a+1$. Thus $2^n+1$ is composite, contradicting the assumption that it is prime. Therefore $n=2^k$ for some integer $k\\ge0$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 65
  },
